# Intent: the device side of on-unit timers and alarms (owner decision 2026-11-13: on-device timers, no Bluetooth, hub down must still work). The schedule is kept in the unit's own flash in a new NVS namespace, mp_timers, key v1 (never the websocket namespace, which holds the pairing token and Wi-Fi); it is read at startXiaozhi and written on every change. The 20 ms stackchan task ticks it every 200 ms against the RTC-backed system clock (hal_rtc.cpp syncs the PCF8563 into the system clock at boot, and the host's server_time sets it first), so a timer fires with no host and after a reboot; an alarm turns HH:MM into the next local occurrence with the unit's timezone. When one is due: a happy face with the reminder card showing its label (the card the upstream reminders use), the ring pulses cyan 0 91 101 (the lights standard's pairing row: cyan at 0.6, a short pulse every 2 s), the existing notification earcon plays at once and every 8 s unless the timer was set silent, the screen is kept awake, the stop word is armed (best effort), and the host gets a timer_fired event (id, what = timer or alarm, label, late; not 'kind', which is the event's own field). The ring ends by itself after one minute (event timer_ended how=timeout); a touch, the stop word or the host end it sooner (patch 093) and the ring goes dark. A timer that came due while the unit was off rings if at most 10 minutes late, else it is dropped and reported as timer_missed. A timer set while the clock was never set runs on boot time and does not survive a reboot. New public device_events_push(kind, extra) is the non-diagnostic event path (the diag path drops events when a minute's budget is used).
# Upstream: new files hal/maipai_timers.h, hal/maipai_timers.cc; hal/hal.cpp, hal/hal_mcp.cpp, hal/device_events.h (MIT). Applies after 090 with --unidiff-zero.
# Check after a flash: boot log has 'MAIPAI-TIMERS: loaded 0 timer(s)'; with patch 092, set a 20 s timer, reboot the unit inside the 20 s, and at 20 s from the set the face smiles with the card, the ring pulses cyan, the notification tone plays every 8 s; a touch on the screen ends it and the ring goes dark; leave one alone and it ends at 60 s on its own; kill Wi-Fi and the hub before the 20 s and it still rings.
